# spline_cap.cam — analytic NURBS reader fixture (ROADMAP Phase 4 follow-up).
# A surface of revolution whose meridian is an arbitrary cubic b-spline
# (meridian.spline), machined by finish_spline_revolve — the *analytic*
# counterpart to mesh.cam. Where the freeform_cap STEP fixture tessellates the
# same kind of revolved-spline face to an STL and the drop-cutter accepts the
# stair-step error (DECISIONS.md "Camp-3 slice 1…"), this reads the meridian as
# an exact curve and leaves NO tessellation error: contact point, surface
# normal, and curvature all come from de Boor evaluation of the spline.
#
# The meridian (see meridian.spline) is a monotone freeform cap — apex on the
# axis at z=20, base rim at r=13, z=0 — that no camp-1/2 equation names, so it
# is recognition's residue and the reader's job. Convex toward the tool over
# its whole span, so the shared gouge guard finds no concave point and (like
# the sphere/ogive noses) does not fire; a concave meridian would be guarded
# against its tightest radius of curvature from the exact second derivative.
#
# Waterline (stepover) finishing here, spaced at uniform meridian arc length;
# the constant-scallop twin of this fixture is spline_cap_scallop.cam (the
# curvature-adaptive Newton march, DECISIONS.md "Constant scallop over a spline
# meridian…"). The committed golden (tests/spline_cap.golden.gcode) plus the
# closed-form nurbs unit tests (the rational quarter-circle reproduces a circle
# to 1e-9) are the anchors.
#
# CONTAINED 2026-08-13 (DECISIONS.md "rim-area accounting…", slice 2):
# the stock used to stop AT this feature's base plane, so the whole
# surface stood in air and the fixture only ran because there was
# nothing to hit. The stock now stands 2mm proud of the feature's top
# (z 22) and T1 roughs the moat before the finish;
# numbers in the older comments describe the same toolpath, whose
# motion is unchanged where it touches the surface.
units mm
feed 600
plunge 200
rapid_z 25
clearance_z 40
spindle 4000

stock Box(w=100, l=80, h=62, x=0, y=0, z=22)

tool T1 Endmill(dia=12.0)
tool T10 Ballmill(dia=6.0)

# The spline cap sits centred at (50, 40); z=0 places its base rim on z=0, apex
# at z=20 (the meridian's own z, added to the feature z).
feature Cap SplineRevolve(file="meridian.spline", x=50, y=40, z=0)

run Clear(tool=T1, profile=Cap, stepover=8.0, stepdown=5.0, stock_to_leave=0.5)
run Copy(tool=T10, surface=Cap, stepover=3.0)
